# OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications (2025)

Treat the model as an untrusted, steerable component. Governance, privacy, and operations for AI systems are covered in the `owasp-ai-security-privacy` rule.

## LLM01 Prompt Injection
- Anything the model reads (user input, web pages, files, emails, tool results, retrieved docs) can carry instructions
- Keep system/developer instructions separate from untrusted content; mark untrusted content clearly; never let it change policy
- Enforce security in code, not in the prompt: authorisation and allowlists must hold even if the model is fully hijacked

## LLM02 Sensitive Information Disclosure
- Send the minimum data needed; redact secrets and PII before prompts, logs, and traces
- Scope retrieval and memory to the caller's permissions and tenant
- Check provider data-retention/training settings; disable training on user data by default

## LLM03 Supply Chain
- Pin model versions, SDKs, MCP servers, and plugins; review before upgrading
- Load models/weights only from trusted sources with verified hashes/signatures; prefer safe formats (e.g. safetensors over pickle)
- No dynamic loading of unreviewed tools or MCP servers

## LLM04 Data and Model Poisoning
- Track provenance for training, fine-tuning, and RAG data; validate before ingestion
- Do not auto-learn or auto-index from user content without review gates

## LLM05 Improper Output Handling
- Never pass model output directly to shell, SQL, `eval`, file paths, HTTP calls, or HTML
- Require structured output; validate against a strict schema; reject on violation (fail closed)
- Encode output for its destination context (HTML, Markdown links/images, URLs)

## LLM06 Excessive Agency
- Give agents the fewest tools, narrowest scopes, and read-only access by default
- Tools act with the end user's permissions, not a broad service identity
- Require human approval for destructive, financial, external-communication, or privileged actions
- Sandbox code execution; restrict filesystem and network egress with allowlists

## LLM07 System Prompt Leakage
- Assume system prompts will be disclosed: no secrets, credentials, internal URLs, or authorisation logic in them

## LLM08 Vector and Embedding Weaknesses
- Enforce access control on vector stores per tenant/user at query time, not just at ingestion
- Validate and sanitise documents before embedding; record source for every chunk

## LLM09 Misinformation
- Ground answers in retrieved sources and cite them; validate facts that drive decisions with deterministic checks
- Communicate uncertainty in UX; never auto-apply model suggestions (e.g. generated code, package names) without verification

## LLM10 Unbounded Consumption
- Cap input/output tokens, tool-call loops, recursion depth, and wall-clock time
- Rate limit and budget per user/key; bounded retries with backoff; alert on cost spikes

## Reviewer checklist
- [ ] Untrusted content separated from instructions; security enforced outside the prompt
- [ ] Model output schema-validated and never executed or rendered raw
- [ ] Tools least-privilege, user-scoped; risky actions need human approval
- [ ] No secrets in prompts; PII redacted from prompts and logs
- [ ] RAG/vector queries filtered by caller permissions
- [ ] Models, SDKs, MCP servers, and plugins pinned and vetted
- [ ] Token, loop, time, rate, and cost limits in place
